    <item rdf:about="http://ec2-13-54-13-106.ap-southeast-2.compute.amazonaws.com/doku.php?id=tingari_by_george_ward_tjungurrayi&amp;rev=1740618776&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2025-02-27T01:12:56+00:00</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>minhthunguyen (minhthunguyen@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>tingari_by_george_ward_tjungurrayi</title>
        <link>http://ec2-13-54-13-106.ap-southeast-2.compute.amazonaws.com/doku.php?id=tingari_by_george_ward_tjungurrayi&amp;rev=1740618776&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Tingari by George Ward Tjungurrayi

“Tingari” by George Ward Tjungurrayi represents the journeys and rituals of the Tingari ancestors, a group of ancestral beings important in Aboriginal tradition. This artwork captures their travels across the land, leaving sacred sites and knowledge behind, which are crucial for guiding initiations and teaching younger generations. The abstract patterns and concentric circles in the piece echo the sacred landscape, blending spiritual stories with the natural g…</description>
    </item>
    <item rdf:about="http://ec2-13-54-13-106.ap-southeast-2.compute.amazonaws.com/doku.php?id=aboriginal_seasons&amp;rev=1740618675&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2025-02-27T01:11:15+00:00</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>minhthunguyen (minhthunguyen@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>aboriginal_seasons</title>
        <link>http://ec2-13-54-13-106.ap-southeast-2.compute.amazonaws.com/doku.php?id=aboriginal_seasons&amp;rev=1740618675&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Understanding Seasonal Knowledge of First Nations Peoples

Introduction: Aboriginal Seasons

First Nations people have deep knowledge about the environment. All the seasons are mainly based on environmental changes rather than specific months. Every season marks important ecological events like plant flowering, animal behavior, and weather patterns.</description>
    </item>
    <item rdf:about="http://ec2-13-54-13-106.ap-southeast-2.compute.amazonaws.com/doku.php?id=tali_by_eubena_nampitjin&amp;rev=1740618399&amp;do=diff">
        <dc:format>text/html</dc:format>
        <dc:date>2025-02-27T01:06:39+00:00</dc:date>
        <dc:creator>Anonymous (anonymous@undisclosed.example.com)</dc:creator>
        <title>tali_by_eubena_nampitjin</title>
        <link>http://ec2-13-54-13-106.ap-southeast-2.compute.amazonaws.com/doku.php?id=tali_by_eubena_nampitjin&amp;rev=1740618399&amp;do=diff</link>
        <description>Tali by Eubena Nampitjin

“Tali” by Eubena Nampitjin depicts the sand dunes, or “tali,” of the Great Sandy Desert, a landscape deeply connected to her heritage. The artwork uses flowing lines and rich earthy tones to represent the movement and patterns of the dunes shaped by wind and time. Through this piece, Nampitjin brings to life her memories and ancestral connections to the desert, highlighting the beauty and spiritual importance of her homeland.</description>
    </item>
